@@ -103,7 +103,7 @@ All data comes from **your own** copy of the pack and world: recipes are exporte
   - **Farms we already run**: Forestry farms and any other farm the group runs, typed in as what each gives a minute. A running line takes their output free up to that rate (a Make N batch takes nothing from them). It is a project setting: enter the group's farm in each project that should use it; each of them may take up to its rate.
   - **What a farm costs** (the user's rulings of 2026-10-20): a farm your group owns makes its output at its upkeep (near nothing for rubber wood); one it could build is a source like any other, priced, wherever build cost counts, by its machine's build and its plantings (crop sticks and their soil) plus its upkeep, never ignored; a farm it already runs is free up to its rate. The raw cap's leanest line counts the farms your group could build (the user's ruling of 2026-10-24): where a farm makes the leanest line leaner, **Raw material for fewer machines** is measured against that, so a plan may build the farm and its chain to stay within the cap.
   - **Layout**: a farm is laid out as a reserved area with a pin and one build step that says what to build or plant there. The 3D view, the Connections view and the ghost projector show no farm blocks, and the line's power and logistics do not reach the farm: what it makes goes into the line's input chest like a raw input.
-- **Other projects' surplus** (*Use other projects' surplus*, on by default, running lines only): a running line takes what the group's other projects make and do not need, up to their surplus, before it buys or makes it. Such a row names the project in Materials (*from Rubber line*), and the banner says what the line draws (*draws Refined Glue from Rubber line*). Several projects taking from one producer are served oldest project first, and two projects never feed each other in a circle. In *Best multi-output* a line is credited for the byproducts of its own runs that other projects need (*for Electronic Circuit* under its byproducts; *Also makes for other projects* on the Compare card), but it never runs more to make them. Where two projects' plans would keep changing what each other can use, the planner stops after one round and says so (*This plan and Rubber line keep changing what each other can use; …*). A Make N batch neither takes nor offers surplus.
+- **Other projects' surplus** (*Use other projects' surplus*, on by default, running lines only): a running line takes what the group's other projects make and do not need, up to their surplus, before it buys or makes it. Such a row names the project in Materials (*from Rubber line*), and the banner says what the line draws (*draws Refined Glue from Rubber line*). A line takes surplus only from projects created before it, and from projects that take none themselves (*Use other projects' surplus* off and not *Best multi-output*), so two projects never feed each other and coming back to a project finds its plan as it left it. Several projects taking from one producer are served oldest project first. In *Best multi-output* a line is credited for the byproducts of its own runs that other projects need (*for Electronic Circuit* under its byproducts; *Also makes for other projects* on the Compare card), but it never runs more to make them. Where two projects' plans would keep changing what each other can use, the planner stops after one round and says so (*This plan and Rubber line keep changing what each other can use; …*). A Make N batch neither takes nor offers surplus.
 
