 - Shared state across the group: ticks, crossed-off steps, notes/pins, recipe choices, material costs, logistics mode, target/tier. Everyone sees the same thing (refresh or live).
 - No accounts: each person types their name once. One shared password keeps strangers out, when the group turns it on (changed 2026-11-03 at the user's request: the password is off by default, and then anyone who can reach the planner's port can use it; `npm run set-password` turns it on, `npm run set-password -- --off` off). A projector's project token is checked either way.
 - **Projects can be deleted** (added 2026-10-18 at the user's request) by anyone in the group, after confirming by typing the project name. A deleted project leaves the list at once (open tabs are told and go back to the list), stays restorable from "Recently deleted" for 7 days, then is removed for good with its notes, ticks, history and projector tokens (my call: restorable, so a mis-click never loses the group's progress).
-- **Surplus across projects** (added 2026-10-19 at the user's request): a project may use what the group's other projects make and do not need, up to their surplus, shown as 'from <project>'; a project planned for best multi-output also counts the byproducts it makes that another project needs. A project never changes another one, and two projects never feed each other in a circle. When several projects use one project's surplus, the oldest project is served first; one that gets less is told so, and the project that makes it lists who takes how much. A project never makes more to serve another: only what its own work makes counts.
+- **Surplus across projects** (added 2026-10-19 at the user's request): a project may use what the group's other projects make and do not need, up to their surplus, shown as 'from <project>'; a project planned for best multi-output also counts the byproducts it makes that another project needs. A project never changes another one, and two projects never feed each other in a circle: a project takes surplus only from projects older than it and from projects that take none themselves (changed 2026-11-04 at the user's request, RUK-529: before, a project could take from any other whose draws did not lead back to it, so the plans kept changing each other on every visit). When several projects use one project's surplus, the oldest project is served first; one that gets less is told so, and the project that makes it lists who takes how much. A project never makes more to serve another: only what its own work makes counts.
 
